    "post": "IKEA replaced 8,500 customer service roles with AI.\n\nAnd the company is now $1.4 billion richer because of it...\n\nBack in 2021, IKEA’s largest franchisee, Ingka, launched an AI assistant called Billie.\n\nWithin two years, Billie was handling 47% of customer queries to its call centres.\n\nThe obvious move would have been to cut headcount.\n\nIKEA went the other way.\n\nIt retrained 8,500 call centre workers as remote interior design advisers.\n\nCustomers book a video call, get help planning a room and leave with a product list.\n\nIn its 2022 financial year, that channel brought in €1.3 billion ($1.4 billion) in sales. Ingka is aiming for it to reach 10% of total revenue by 2028.\n\nIt’s one of the smartest uses of AI I’ve seen.\n\nTo sum it up, IKEA decided to:\n\n✅ Use AI to clear the repetitive work.\n✅ Redeploy people to the work AI can’t do.\n✅ Build a new service line around what those people do best.\n\nThat’s how you build defensibility in 2026.\n\nAI is already changing how we work. The real question is where you put your people once it does.\n\nBe honest about what AI does better. \nBe ruthless about putting your people where they create the most leverage.\n\nAI can handle the standardised tasks.\n\nHumans belong where judgement, taste and relationships matter.\n\nPlenty of companies will treat AI as a way to cut costs and stop there.\n\nThe ones that follow IKEA’s example will build something far harder to copy.\n\nLove seeing stories like these, and I hope to read many more in the future.\n\nEven though AI can feel doom and gloom to some folks, I hope this provides you a different perspective.",
