    "post": "Carole Hopson was an HR executive at Foot Locker and L’Oréal. At 50, she became an airline pilot. She is now a Boeing 737 Captain at United Airlines, and she says she is one of fewer than 150 Black women professional pilots in the US.\n\nAs a former VP of HR, I have been a little obsessed with her story since I came across her on socials. \n\nHere’s how she got there:\n→ Police reporter at the Bergen Record, then a master’s from Columbia\n→ NFL Player Programs Coordinator\n→ VP of Training & Development at Foot Locker\n→ Director of HR at L’Oréal\n→ Flight training in 2000, then flight instructor\n\n(she was a flight instructor BEFORE she became a pilot for a major airline) \n\n→ Airline pilot at 50\n→ United Airlines Captain\n→ Author of A Pair of Wings, inspired by Bessie Coleman. \n\nI recently purchased her book, please check it out! \n\nWhat she shares on The Tamsin Show:\n→ The deadline that turned her childhood dream into a real goal\n→ How she prepared financially for a major career change\n→ Why she waited until her sons were older to fly professionally\n→ What it felt like to be the oldest person in the classroom\n→ How to build a personal “board of directors” for your next chapter\n\nWhatever your “someday” is, start building toward it now.\n\nStarting over at 50 is possible, heck we're all starting over all the time no matter our ages. \n\nHear her story on The Tamsen Show and read A Pair of Wings.\n\nSalute, Captain Hopson!\n\n---\n\nShare this with someone who needs a reminder it's not too late.",
