    "width": 720,
    "height": 900,
    "post": "I've been building businesses since I was 21 (35 now)\n\nIf I had to relearn all my knowledge again, I'd start here:\n\nTo be honest, a lot of business books are one good idea stretched over 200 pages.\n\nA few changed my businesses, and a handful I disagree with completely:\n\nThe Best Of The Best 🏅\n\nAtomic Habits: If you only read one book this year, make it this one, because your results end up as good as the systems you build.\n\nThe 4-Hour Workweek: Probably my favourite book ever, and the real lesson is leverage.\n\nGood to Great: Still the best breakdown I've read of what makes a business exceptional.\n\nShoe Dog: The Nike Swoosh cost $30, and Phil Knight's story is the best reminder I know that great businesses look messy at the start.\n\nKey Person of Influence: It's a big reason I take personal branding so seriously.\n\nRead At Least Once 🥈\n\nThe Lean Startup: Essential if you're unsure whether to start, or you've lost touch with what customers want.\n\nZero to One: A good challenge for anyone in a crowded market to check whether they're doing anything different at all.\n\nDeep Work: Whenever my calendar fills up and my focus goes, this is the book I go back to.\n\nThe Psychology of Money: It taught me that money decisions are mostly emotional, which helped me stop making reactive ones.\n\nThe Hard Thing About Hard Things: The most honest book I've read on what to do when every option in front of you is a bad one.\n\nGood For Specific People 🥉\n\nStart With Why: Useful when you're hiring and rallying a team, but early on I'd put that energy into getting moving and making money.\n\nBlue Ocean Strategy: One big reframe that's worth knowing if you're stuck competing on price.\n\nRich Dad Poor Dad: It got me thinking about owning assets instead of renting out my time.\n\nThink and Grow Rich: It helped me set a clear goal in my 20s, when I was working hard and getting nowhere.\n\nHug Your Haters: I took one lesson from it, which is that complaints handled well can turn into your most loyal customers.\n\nNot For Me ❌\n\nEgo Is the Enemy: There's good stuff on humility, but keeping quiet about your work would have cost me a lot of the opportunities I've had.\n\nCompany of One: Staying small is a valid choice, but it goes against how I've built every business I've run.\n\nDigital Minimalism: I love Deep Work, but social media has opened more doors for me than anything else.\n\nInbound Marketing: Being found matters more than ever, but our SDRs still make 100 outbound touches a day for a reason.\n\nThe 10",
