    "post": "If you earned €100,000 a year, how much would actually land in your bank account?\n\nAcross Europe, the answer varies by more than €36,000.\n\nAfter income tax and employee social contributions:\n\n🇧🇬 Bulgaria: €86,930\n🇪🇪 Estonia: €74,400\n🇨🇿 Czechia: €72,800\n🇲🇹 Malta: €72,500\n🇨🇭 Switzerland: €70,500\n🇨🇾 Cyprus: €70,300\n🇬🇧 United Kingdom: €69,900\n🇸🇰 Slovakia: €67,855\n🇳🇴 Norway: €66,900\n🇭🇺 Hungary: €66,500\n🇱🇻 Latvia: €66,200\n🇪🇸 Spain: €64,200\n🇮🇪 Ireland: €64,000\n🇹🇷 Turkey: €63,200\n🇫🇷 France: €63,000\n🇫🇮 Finland: €62,200\n🇱🇺 Luxembourg: €61,500\n🇭🇷 Croatia: €61,000\n🇱🇹 Lithuania: €60,500\n🇳🇱 Netherlands: €60,500\n🇵🇱 Poland: €60,225\n🇷🇴 Romania: €58,500\n🇩🇪 Germany: €57,900\n🇵🇹 Portugal: €57,000\n🇮🇹 Italy: €56,700\n🇬🇷 Greece: €56,615\n🇸🇮 Slovenia: €55,060\n🇦🇹 Austria: €54,200\n🇸🇪 Sweden: €52,000\n🇩🇰 Denmark: €51,500\n🇧🇪 Belgium: €50,750\n\nSame €100,000 salary.\n\nIn Bulgaria, you keep almost €87,000.\nIn Belgium, just over €50,000.\n\nThat's a difference of €36,180 a year from the exact same gross salary.\n\nAnd this is before even comparing:\n\n→ Cost of living\n→ Housing\n→ Healthcare\n→ Childcare\n→ Pensions\n→ What you actually receive in public services\n\nGross salary only tells you part of the story.\n\nWhere you earn it matters too 🌎\n\nWhich country surprises you most??\n\nNeed help to build your personal brand and audience on LinkedIn? Talk soon: https://hunt.rs/ 👋",
